      "applies_when": [],
      "summary": "`UserDefaults`, `@AppStorage`, and React Native `AsyncStorage` write unencrypted plists or files that land in device backups and are readable on a compromised device. Session tokens, refresh tokens, passwords, and API keys stored there break 1.6's requirement to protect user information from unauthorized access.",
      "fix": "Store secrets in the keychain with an accessibility class such as `kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly`; in React Native use a keychain-backed module. Keep only non-sensitive preferences in `UserDefaults`.",
      "references": [
        "https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/keychain-services"
      ],
      "check": {
        "id": "source-patterns",
        "confidence": "medium",
        "patterns": [
          "(?i)@AppStorage\\(\\s*\"[^\"]*(token|password|passwd|secret|api_?key|jwt|session)[^\"]*\"",
          "(?i)UserDefaults[^\\n]*\\.set\\([^\\n]*forKey:\\s*\"[^\"]*(token|password|passwd|secret|api_?key|jwt|session)",
          "(?i)\\[\\[NSUserDefaults standardUserDefaults\\] setObject:[^\\n]*forKey:@\"[^\"]*(token|password|secret|apikey|jwt)",
          "(?i)AsyncStorage\\.setItem\\(\\s*[\"'`][^\"'`]*(token|password|secret|api_?key|jwt|session)"
        ]
      },
      "review": [
        "Where does the app keep session tokens, refresh tokens, and passwords, and is it the keychain?"
